WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.038
-Joining us right now is the
host of "The Reidout" on MSNBC,

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:07.406
which airs weekdays at 7:00 p.m.

00:00:07.406 --> 00:00:09.642
Please welcome Joy Reid!

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:10.910
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.912
Joy!

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.747
Thank you so much.

00:00:14.747 --> 00:00:17.150
Thank you so much
for joining us.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:19.486
I always love it
when you come on the show.

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:22.354
Today we learned the verdict
of the trial.

00:00:22.354 --> 00:00:25.858
Derek Chauvin was found guilty
on all three charges.

00:00:25.858 --> 00:00:28.127
Can you explain, like --

00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:30.396
walk us through the charges
and what they mean?

00:00:30.396 --> 00:00:32.332
-Absolutely. Well, first of all,
thank you, Jimmy.

00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:34.400
It's always great
to be on with you.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.671
So, basically, Derek Chauvin
faced second-degree murder,

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:41.474
third-degree murder,
and manslaughter charges.

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:45.478
And those sentences potentially
go all the way from about --

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:47.747
almost 50 years
for second-degree murder,

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:50.349
which is intentional murder,

00:00:50.349 --> 00:00:51.917
down to manslaughter,

00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:54.387
which is basically sort of
enhanced negligence,

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:58.091
just behaving negligently
in a way that caused death.

00:00:58.091 --> 00:00:59.458
He got them all.

00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:02.128
He was found guilty
on all three.

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:05.731
And I can tell you, Jimmy, the
time that it became pretty clear

00:01:05.731 --> 00:01:09.101
to me that he was likely to be
convicted is when we heard

00:01:09.101 --> 00:01:13.205
there was a verdict and the jury
had only been out for 11 hours.

00:01:13.205 --> 00:01:16.575
And that's about the time
that the Mohamed Noor jury,

00:01:16.575 --> 00:01:17.744
which was another
police officer,

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.445
a black police officer
in Minneapolis

00:01:19.445 --> 00:01:22.615
who was convicted of
second-degree murder --

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.383
they were out
for about 11 hours.

00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:27.753
So a very quick return
of a verdict

00:01:27.753 --> 00:01:30.189
kind of indicated that it was
going to be guilty.

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:31.790
-Yeah. I mean,

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:35.328
I don't know what it was like
for you in the studio.

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:38.297
But for me, I'm just
shaking the whole day.

00:01:38.297 --> 00:01:43.670
And I go, "Well, I've seen
the craziest stuff happen,"

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:47.206
for a second, and you go --
I mean, were you at all going,

00:01:47.206 --> 00:01:48.842
"I wouldn't be surprised if..."

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:50.175
-I have to tell you, Jimmy,

00:01:50.175 --> 00:01:53.278
I'm very, very cynical about
charges against police.

00:01:53.278 --> 00:01:55.815
Right? You can go all the way
back to Amadou Diallo,

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:57.549
who was shot
in his own vestibule.

00:01:57.549 --> 00:01:59.719
And this was, I think, in 1999.

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:02.655
An African immigrant shot dead
by the police.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:04.023
No charges.

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:06.859
You can go to Patrick Dorismond,
who was killed in New York

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:09.428
around that same period
where Rudolph Giuliani,

00:02:09.428 --> 00:02:11.230
when he was mayor,
released his juvenile record

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:14.567
to justify him being killed by
an undercover police officer.

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:16.369
Go all the way through --
Walter Scott,

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:19.004
the case where you saw a man
running in South Carolina

00:02:19.004 --> 00:02:21.040
from a police officer,
and he shoots him dead,

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.308
shoots him in the back.

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.877
That jury hung,
and it was on tape as well.

00:02:24.877 --> 00:02:26.846
You can just go through it.
Michael Brown.

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:29.515
You can go through Tamir Rice,
who was a 12-year-old child

00:02:29.515 --> 00:02:30.749
playing with a toy gun.

00:02:30.749 --> 00:02:32.852
Police officer jumps out,
shoots him immediately.

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:34.120
No charges.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.056
So I tend to go with
the statistics that show --

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:38.591
what? -- about 2% of
police officers

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:40.793
are ever convicted
of killing someone,

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:42.462
let alone killing
somebody black.

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:45.498
So I tend to think, when
a police officer is charged,

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:49.102
I steel myself for them
to not be charged.

00:02:49.102 --> 00:02:50.370
And then if they're charged,

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.039
I steel myself emotionally
for them to be acquitted,

00:02:53.039 --> 00:02:55.041
'cause that's normally
what happens.

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:59.411
So the fact that this was
such a lopsided case,

00:02:59.411 --> 00:03:02.948
watching it every day,
the quality of the prosecution

00:03:02.948 --> 00:03:08.154
versus the sort of phoned-in,
strange quality of the defense,

00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:11.323
even that doesn't guarantee
a conviction when it's police.

00:03:11.323 --> 00:03:12.791
-Right.
-But the fact that I think

00:03:12.791 --> 00:03:17.329
you had 10 police officers,
including the police chief,

00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:20.433
testify against this officer,
which I've never seen,

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:24.337
the fact you had so much
unanimity of police experts

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:26.973
testifying against
this police officer,

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:28.774
it did feel like the momentum

00:03:28.774 --> 00:03:30.676
was going in
the prosecution's direction.

00:03:30.676 --> 00:03:32.578
But I didn't dare assume

00:03:32.578 --> 00:03:33.779
that that was going to
be the outcome.

00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:35.113
-Right.

00:03:35.113 --> 00:03:38.517
It's hard to imagine
what the family of George Floyd

00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:41.854
is going through right now,
obviously.

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:45.024
But there's got to be
some feeling

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:48.160
that there's been justice.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.196
-Absolutely. This is so rare.

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:52.999
I mean, again, Jimmy,
think about how rare it is

00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:55.968
for a police officer
to be convicted of

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:59.572
killing a black man, woman,
or child in America.

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:01.207
For this family,

00:04:01.207 --> 00:04:04.544
they have achieved something
almost revolutionary.

00:04:04.544 --> 00:04:08.014
They have not only been able
to get justice for themselves,

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:09.615
which isn't going to
ease their pain.

00:04:09.615 --> 00:04:11.384
They don't have their brother,

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:13.185
their family member,
their loved one.

00:04:13.185 --> 00:04:14.454
He's gone.

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:16.855
But they've achieved justice
that is going to radiate

00:04:16.855 --> 00:04:18.858
potentially
throughout our system,

00:04:18.858 --> 00:04:22.094
because the defense that was
put on for Derek Chauvin

00:04:22.094 --> 00:04:24.764
was the same defense that was
put on in the Rodney King case.

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:26.365
"He had super-human strength.

00:04:26.365 --> 00:04:28.301
He was on drugs, therefore
he was going to get up,

00:04:28.301 --> 00:04:29.469
really rise from the dead,"

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:30.970
'cause he was dead
for about three minutes

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:34.873
of Derek Chauvin having his knee
and his full body weight on him,

00:04:34.873 --> 00:04:37.076
that he was going to rise from
the dead and still be a threat.

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:38.411
"The black man
is always a threat.

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:40.379
The black body is a threat."

00:04:40.379 --> 00:04:42.281
He tried everything.
"He was on drugs.

00:04:42.281 --> 00:04:45.984
He was, you know, the real
culprit in his own death."

00:04:45.984 --> 00:04:47.653
And that didn't work.

00:04:47.653 --> 00:04:50.256
And so I think what's happened
is that we've established

00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:52.825
a line that was drawn by police,

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:55.994
a line that was drawn
in the criminal justice system,

00:04:55.994 --> 00:04:59.032
that you just can't kill a man
in front of witnesses,

00:04:59.032 --> 00:05:04.236
including a 9-year-old, on video
camera and walk away from it.

00:05:04.236 --> 00:05:05.972
I think it's important
that we draw that line

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:07.006
because law enforcement

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:10.042
is losing the consent
of the governed.

00:05:10.042 --> 00:05:12.911
They're losing credibility with
the American people,

00:05:12.911 --> 00:05:14.681
particularly in communities
of color.

00:05:14.681 --> 00:05:17.716
This needed to go this way,
in a way, just for our society

00:05:17.716 --> 00:05:19.818
to be able to take
a step forward.

00:05:19.818 --> 00:05:23.389
-I thought it was interesting,
too, after the verdict

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:27.126
was announced, seeing different
locations and people's reaction.

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:30.696
It wasn't so much -- It wasn't
celebration or people jumping.

00:05:30.696 --> 00:05:33.766
It was just people going...
and just taking a beat.

00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:36.168
And that was like, wow.
That was powerful.

00:05:36.168 --> 00:05:37.770
-Yeah, there's nothing
to celebrate

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.138
when someone goes to prison,
right?

00:05:39.138 --> 00:05:40.706
This man's life is over --
Derek Chauvin.

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:44.576
He made a decision, and he had
9 minutes and 29 seconds

00:05:44.576 --> 00:05:47.213
to change his mind
and make a different choice.

00:05:47.213 --> 00:05:50.816
The decision he made
is the end of his career.

00:05:50.816 --> 00:05:52.318
It's the end, maybe,

00:05:52.318 --> 00:05:53.886
of his freedom
for the rest of his life.

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:56.054
We don't know what kind of
a sentence he's going to get.

00:05:56.054 --> 00:05:58.257
He has tainted law enforcement.

00:05:58.257 --> 00:06:00.826
He has embarrassed
and shamed his department.

00:06:00.826 --> 00:06:03.329
And that was what
the testimony was,

00:06:03.329 --> 00:06:05.064
that this was an embarrassment

00:06:05.064 --> 00:06:06.498
to what law enforcement
is supposed to be.

00:06:06.498 --> 00:06:08.134
Law enforcement people
that I know

00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:09.968
were embarrassed by what he did.

00:06:09.968 --> 00:06:11.871
He shamed himself
and he's lost his freedom.

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:13.372
There's nothing to celebrate.

00:06:13.372 --> 00:06:17.576
But I do think that for
the family it's a relief, right?

00:06:17.576 --> 00:06:19.812
I mean, there were people
who were interviewed by

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:21.814
Shaq Brewster, who's
our reporter on the ground,

00:06:21.814 --> 00:06:23.249
who said, "I can breathe now."

00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:25.351
People can stop
holding their breath.

00:06:25.351 --> 00:06:27.819
And I think just for a moment
we can acknowledge

00:06:27.819 --> 00:06:29.955
that that is -- it is a relief.

00:06:29.955 --> 00:06:31.791
It isn't anything
to be joyful about.

00:06:31.791 --> 00:06:34.593
-Joy, thank you so much
for coming on to speak to us.

00:06:34.593 --> 00:06:35.761
I always appreciate it.

00:06:35.761 --> 00:06:36.896
Joy Reid, everyone.

